  "id": "Compound::DB00231 - Gene::2560",
  "verbalization": "Temazepam, a benzodiazepine, is shown to bind to Gabrr3 or Gabrg1, both of which are subunits of Gaba receptor complexes. These receptors are involved in modulating Gaba receptor activity, which is a key mechanism in the regulation of neuronal excitation. The evidence suggests that Temazepam exerts its effects through a common pathway involving Gaba receptor activity, with Gabrb1 being a downstream target of this activity. The integration of multiple paths highlights a potential mechanism where Temazepam interacts with Gaba receptor subunits, leading to an increase in Gaba receptor activity, which in turn may influence neuronal function. The shared involvement of Gaba receptor activity across different binding sites implies a broader role for these receptors in the pharmacological effects of Temazepam.",
